I'll start with the review criteria and the essay, then check Lem quotes against the corpus.Next I’ll check the chapter text, quote rows, and 2026 claims against the essay’s ledger.I’ll pull the chapter text and verify the quotes the essay leans on.Dumping the chapter pages and endnote so I can check quotes, hedges, and omitted context.The endnote numbering looks off. I’ll check the chapter body and remaining pages next.Checking later-Lem, quote pages, and the 2026 receipts next.Verifying the 2026 receipts and a few quote-page details I still doubt.Checking one more quote-page pairing and whether note 6 is attached where the essay implies.# Essay 2 review — findings only

## 1. The “legislature” indictment is a misread of the unread half of the chapter

**Location:** “Nothing in the two-evolutions model has a place to put that”; “his frame has only one selection pressure. Everything in the chapter happens by competition in a niche.”

**Problem:** Two pages after the similarity sentence the essay treats as the thesis, Lem writes that “technical evolution is not, for the time being at least, people-free” and that “the biggest difference” is that bioevolution is “an amoral process, which is something we cannot say about technical evolution” (p. 38). He then spends the rest of the chapter on human agency: moral choice and irreversibility (pp. 47–49), states accelerating or stalling a technology by spending (p. 51), and a civilisation saying “Enough!” and refusing to automatize (p. 57). Regulation as a selector is a 2026 specification of a difference he named and developed. The essay never quotes past “Similarities.”

**Fix:** Drop or rewrite the “nowhere to put it” claim. If the political selector is the essay’s negative, score it as a problem-level row against p. 38’s “biggest difference” (he named agency; he did not model a legislature that kills the fit and saves the unfit) — not as a hole in a chapter the essay did not read.

---

## 2. The air-cushion MISS grades a hedged illustration as a dated forecast

**Location:** Lede: “he was rash enough to date it”; ledger row: `"further development of mechanical vehicles, which will make wheel drive obsolete"`; “Where he was worst”: “He picked the wrong successor.”

**Problem:** The governing verbs are “One can also imagine” and “it is quite likely that, say” (p. 36). He gives no year. Two sentences earlier he says cars and aeroplanes “have not yet reached their developmental peak, which is why we cannot talk about their late forms” (p. 35). The lawnmower is a worked example of the relic *pattern*, contingent on a successor that would abolish the wheel. The ledger strips the hedges; the lede invents a dating. Same class of error as scoring a reductio as a forecast (essay 4) and as the hedged entertainment application that had to be pulled from MISS to OPEN (essay 6).

**Fix:** Either keep a narrow object-level MISS on “will make wheel drive obsolete” with the hedges in the Lem cell and stop calling it dated, or move the successor to prose and let the relic row (already OPEN) carry the pattern. Do not rest “Where he was worst” on a thought-experiment.

---

## 3. Radio HIT is paid by a different event than the one Lem described

**Location:** Ledger: squeezed by television, radio answers with “a sudden ‘radiation of variations’… and to their appearance in new ‘ecological niches’” → AM dropped from EVs, 2023.

**Problem:** Lem’s claim is diversification under television pressure (pocket sets, hi-fi, stereophonic sound — p. 36). The receipt is one band losing a habitat to electromagnetic interference, then a lobby. That is contraction plus legislation, not radiation into niches. The AM story belongs in the legislature paragraph, not as payment for this HIT.

**Fix:** Pay the HIT with what actually continued the radiation (transistor/Walkman, FM, satellite, podcasts/streaming) or drop the row. Keep AM-in-EVs as the political-selector case.

---

## 4. The morphological-pattern HIT launders the object-level MISS and OPEN

**Location:** Problem-level row: “Holds across the cases he named and the ones he could not — the pattern is doing real work, not decorating a metaphor.”

**Problem:** The pattern as stated is four parts: bad copying prototypes, radiation, gigantism-before-decline, relics in lateral branches (pp. 33–36). Gigantism is untested in 2026. The relic branch is the essay’s own OPEN and is failing from the wrong end. The successor the pattern was asked to name is the essay’s MISS. The receipts are VTOL, AM-radio (mismatch, finding 3), and EV share (the conservative substitution the essay says he failed to predict). “He got the idea” is paying for “he got the thing wrong.”

**Fix:** Split or narrow. A problem-level HIT can cover only the parts that survived contact (copying prototypes; radiation into niches), with gigantism and relics left OPEN or unscored. Do not cite EV share as evidence the pattern holds.

---

## 5. “Note 1” is note 6, attached at p. 47, and does not recant the analogy

**Location:** “And then, in a footnote, he takes a good deal of it back. Note 1 (p. 356)”; Reading: “Read note 1 (pp. 355–357).”

**Problem:** In the 2013 text the hybridization/tree-model note is **note 6** (`resources/text/summa.txt` p. 355: “6 Interesting results…”), superscripted on “still ahead of us” at p. 47 — the theory-versus-empiricism close of *Differences*, not the set piece. Notes 1–3 of the chapter are Zylinska’s. The note says a tree of technical evolution would “most likely resemble” a biological one, *then* names hybridization as “the difficulty.” That is a qualification, not a taking-back.

**Fix:** Cite note 6 (pp. 355–357), say where it is attached, and replace “takes a good deal of it back” with what the note actually does.

---

## 6. Contract material skipped — including the honest unused miss

**Location:** Whole essay. CONTEXT §6 job: “the bio/techno-evolution parallel **and** his ‘several naïve questions.’” Test: designed vs evolved (scaling laws, emergent capabilities, unplanned stack).

**Problem:** The essay grades pp. 33–38 plus one endnote. It does not mention “Several Naïve Questions” (pp. 55–58), *Differences*, or *The First Cause*. That is where the chapter’s checkable 2026 sentences sit: only non-creative mental labour can be automated — “there is none, and what is more, there can be none” (p. 56); machines “will not need programmers anymore” (p. 57); “‘cleverer’ does not yet mean ‘rebellious’” (p. 57). It is also where the unused object-level miss is: “the universality of our technologies is minimal… it has only been creating narrowly specialized devices” (p. 43) — written before the general-purpose computer became the stack. If finding 2 withdraws the air-cushion MISS, the essay has no honest object-level negative unless it takes this one.

**Fix:** Either add rows (p. 43 specialisation; at least one naïve-question row) or amend §6 to the 2-A scope and say in the essay what was left to another piece. Do not leave p. 43 on the cutting-room floor while calling the air-cushion the chapter’s miss.

---

## 7. 1974 text graded as 1964 observation

**Location:** Kicker and baseline: “The mid-1960s baseline he wrote from”; VTOL HIT paid by Harrier 1969 / V-22 2007 / F-35B 2015.

**Problem:** Zylinska pp. 20–21: the translation is the 1974 fourth edition, “to which Lem made some alterations.” By 1974 the Harrier had been in service five years and the car-carrying cross-Channel hovercraft for six. Scoring Harrier 1969 as fulfillment of a “prototype” sentence he left standing is grading later-Lem for a fact already in the world he last edited. Essay 8 flagged the edition date; this one does not. 1982/1991 are unmentioned (they do not retract this chapter, which should be said).

**Fix:** Date the text as 1974. Treat P.1127/Kestrel (1960) as the 1964 baseline he was watching; treat post-1974 persistence (V-22, F-35B) as the 2026 test. One sentence on the later self-grades: they update other examples, not this analogy.

---

## 8. COBOL row is an unlabeled transposition paid by vendor estimates

**Location:** “Not for software, where there is no extinction: COBOL… 220–800 billion lines”; “by widely quoted figures, most American ATM transactions.”

**Problem:** The p. 38 sentence is about “material processes” illustrated by cars, planes, radios, zeppelins. Applying “similar dynamic laws” to source code is the essay’s move; essay 6 was required to label that kind of move as the essay’s. The numbers are not a current range: ~220 billion is Gartner/Reuters (1990s/2017); ~800 billion is Micro Focus’s 2022 survey (they sell COBOL tools). “Most American ATM transactions” is the Reuters 95% figure. The register already says “industry estimates, not audited”; the prose does not.

**Fix:** Label the software test as the essay’s application of “similar dynamic laws.” Receipt the persistence claim as “still in core banking, on industry estimates,” without the line-count span or the ATM percentage unless a non-vendor source is found.

---

## 9. Wrong pages on two load-bearing quotes

**Location:** “a number of primitive features, as if their birth had been hurried” (p. 33); “bursts into evolutionary radiation” (p. 33).

**Problem:** Both are p. 34. `checks/quotes.tsv` already has them on 34; the HTML does not. `verify.sh` cannot catch a wrong page in the essay if the TSV page is right.

**Fix:** p. 34 both times.

---

## 10. 2026 claims to challenge

All factual present-tense claims in “What happened” carry a `<!-- CHECK -->`. These are the ones that overstate what the receipt is.

| id | Challenge |
|---|---|
| `cobol-production` | Line counts and ATM share are marketing estimates, not a census. Prose treats them as scale. |
| `sore-ban-2024` | Rule is California, new manufacture, spark-ignition ≤25 hp; existing and second-hand stay legal; electric mowers are compliant. Lede “The lawnmower is being banned” is false. |
| `hovercraft-2000` | Lede “The air-cushion vehicle is gone” contradicts the essay’s own “one short scheduled route” (Hovertravel Southsea–Ryde). |
| `am-radio-ev` | 296 House cosponsors was mid-September 2025 (CarPro / NAB). H.R. 979 is at 317 as of this review. “Where he was worst” restates “296” with no marker. Senate “more than sixty” matches S. 315’s 60. Bill is not law — essay is careful in “What happened,” less so later. |
| `alexnet-2012` | True that AlexNet ran on two GTX 580s. “The dominant technology of 2026 is exactly such a hybrid” and “the founding result of deep learning” stretch one 2012 paper across a general law (acceleration + “sudden turns”). |
| `baseline-1964-ch2` | Car-carrying cross-Channel hovercraft entered service in 1968 (SR.N4), not in the mid-1960s baseline of a 1964 chapter. |
| `ev-share-2025` | IEA *Global EV Outlook 2026* matches: 25% global 2025, ~55% China. “Three-quarters… still burn petrol” ignores that IEA “electric” is BEV+PHEV and PHEVs burn petrol. Directionally fine; slightly loose. |
| `vtol-aircraft` / `evtol-2026` | Programme dates and “no US type certificate” hold as of mid-2026 (Joby stage 4 / TIA March 2026; Archer phase 3). Not challenged. |

**Fix:** Bound every lede/worst restatement to the register’s actual scope; put a CHECK on the 296 figure or drop the number; stop using AlexNet as a synonym for 2026 AI.

---

## 11. VTOL HIT is mostly a then-current observation

**Location:** “Where he was watching an arms race in progress he was right.”

**Problem:** “Creating a prototype of the winged plane… capable of taking off and landing vertically” (p. 35) describes the P.1127 (hover 1960), which the baseline row already knows. HIT needs a 2026 event. Persistence in a niche (Harrier family, V-22, F-35B; helicopter not displaced) is that event; “he predicted VTOL” is not.

**Fix:** Word the Lem cell as the defence-of-niche mechanism, the 2026 cell as post-1964/post-1974 persistence. Move civil eVTOL to prose or EARLY; he did not claim a civil type certificate.

---

## 12. Reader

A skeptic of “Lem predicted everything” is not served by this ledger.

The lede calls the analogy “the best sustained analogy in the book” and says he “was rash enough to date” a sentence he hedged and did not date. “Where Lem was better than us” is a method eulogy. The problem-level HIT that “the pattern is doing real work” is the row that cannot be cashed from the object-level rows beneath it.

What would persuade is already almost on the page, if scored honestly: wheels were not abolished; the relic is being killed by statute before the main line; software does not go extinct; and — unread — he said our devices would stay narrowly specialised. Those four sentences survive a hostile reading. The present ledger does not.
**1. Verdict inflation: Grading a description of the present as a prediction (Severity: Critical)**
- **Location:** Ledger row 3 (VTOL) and prose ("Then a live prediction about an arms race in progress: 'just as the predator's strategy...'").
- **Problem:** Lem is describing an existing prototype in the present tense ("It does this by creating a prototype..."). As the essay's own `baseline-1964-ch2` check acknowledges, the first vectored-thrust jet (Hawker P.1127) was already hovering in 1960, four years before the book's publication. Scoring the description of an existing 1960s prototype as a successful 2026 forecast (HIT) is pure verdict inflation.
- **Fix:** Remove this from the ledger, or reclassify it as a correct description of contemporary events rather than a forecast.

**2. Verdict inflation: Crediting a vague idea with a specific outcome (Severity: Critical)**
- **Location:** Ledger row 6 (Note 1) and prose ("The dominant technology of 2026 is exactly such a hybrid: the founding result of deep learning was trained on consumer graphics cards...").
- **Problem:** Lem's footnote predicts that technoevolution will have "sudden turns—which are totally unexpected and unpredictable." The essay awards a HIT because AlexNet (2012) crossing statistics with GPUs was unexpected. Grading a prediction that "unpredictable things will happen" as a HIT when a specific unpredictable thing occurs is exactly the "astrology" the book's rules prohibit.
- **Fix:** Grade the general observation about cross-fertilization as the HIT, or change the verdict to RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON if pinning it specifically to AI. Do not award a clean HIT to a prediction of unpredictability.

**3. Misreading Lem: Treating a hypothetical illustration as a hard prediction (Severity: High)**
- **Location:** Ledger row 2 (Lawnmower) and prose ("which is why the chapter's most datable prediction is going wrong..."). 
- **Problem:** Lem's text says: "it is quite likely that, *say*, a small combustion-driven lawnmower will be the last descendant...". The word "say" explicitly marks this as a hypothetical example of what a relic *would* look like, not a hard forecast that lawnmowers specifically will outlast cars. The essay subjects a rhetorical illustration to a literalistic grading against California's 2024 SORE ban. 
- **Fix:** Demote this from a graded prediction. Either score the general concept of a relic, or acknowledge in the prose that the lawnmower was merely a rhetorical illustration. 

**4. Misreading Lem / Mixing Layers: The Radio Row (Severity: High)**
- **Location:** Ledger row 4 (Radio) and prose ("radio answers with 'a sudden "radiation of variations"...'").
- **Problem:** Lem was describing a *past* event—how the transistor allowed radio to escape TV by radiating into new forms (portable, car radios). The corpus text uses the past tense: "competition from television *led to* a sudden radiation." The essay obscures this by paraphrasing it into the present tense ("answers with") and grades it a HIT by citing a 2023 event where electric cars are dropping AM radio. This mixes a 1950s object-level description with a 2023 problem-level event. Furthermore, dropping a feature is habitat loss (extinction), not a "radiation of variations."
- **Fix:** Cut the row. Lem was describing the past, and the 2023 event contradicts the phenomenon he named.

**5. Overstated 2026 Claim: Software Extinction (Severity: Medium)**
- **Location:** Prose section "What happened" and Ledger row 7 ("Not for software, where there is no extinction").
- **Problem:** The essay points to the survival of COBOL to claim "software does not go extinct." This is a massive overstatement. While COBOL survives in core banking, thousands of software species, platforms, and languages (Flash, Silverlight, OS/2, etc.) are entirely extinct. Finding one survivor does not prove the absence of extinction.
- **Fix:** Soften the claim. State that "extinction in software is not guaranteed" or that "unlike biology, software relics can survive in the centre," rather than claiming absolute non-extinction.

**6. Misreading Lem: The Archaeopteryx Rule (Severity: Low)**
- **Location:** Prose section "Where he was worst" ("He applied that rule to the archaeopteryx and forgot it for the car.").
- **Problem:** The essay claims Lem applied the rule that "evolution is a tinkerer... and a lineage under pressure does not reinvent its body plan" to the archaeopteryx. He did not. He merely used the archaeopteryx to say prototypes are clumsy ("did not as much fly as flutter"). The "tinkerer" concept belongs to François Jacob (1977), not Lem.
- **Fix:** Cut the claim that Lem applied this specific rule to the archaeopteryx. 

**7. Reader Persuasion (Severity: Low)**
- **Location:** The essay's overall rhetorical stance.
- **Problem:** A skeptic would not be persuaded by the ledger. The uneven strictness—aggressively penalizing a hypothetical ("say, a lawnmower") while generously rewarding a prediction of unpredictability (AlexNet) and a description of an existing prototype (VTOL)—makes the grading feel rigged to hit a quota rather than objectively testing Lem's claims.
- **Fix:** Implementing the fixes above will harmonize the strictness and restore the ledger's credibility.
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Search the Summa corpus and print page + section for every hit.

    scripts/find.py "intelligence amplifier"
    scripts/find.py -p 107            # dump printed page 107
    scripts/find.py -s "The Black Box" # dump one section

Use this to write checks/quotes.tsv rows: the page it prints is the page the
row must cite. Matching is whitespace- and quote-mark-insensitive, like verify.sh.
"""
import re, sys
from corpus import load, norm

pages, sections = load()

def page_section(n):
    cur = "?"
    for start, title in sections:
        if start <= n: cur = title
        else: break
    return cur

if len(sys.argv) < 2:
    print(__doc__); sys.exit(0)
if sys.argv[1] == "-p":
    n = int(sys.argv[2]); print("== p.%d [%s]" % (n, page_section(n))); print(pages[n]); sys.exit(0)
if sys.argv[1] == "-s":
    want = sys.argv[2].lower()
    for i, (start, title) in enumerate(sections):
        if title.lower() == want:
            end = sections[i + 1][0] if i + 1 < len(sections) else len(pages)
            for n in range(start, end):
                print("== p.%d" % n); print(pages[n])
            sys.exit(0)
    print("no section named", sys.argv[2]); sys.exit(1)

needle = norm(" ".join(sys.argv[1:]))
hits = 0
for n, text in pages.items():
    flat = norm(text)
    i = flat.find(needle)
    while i >= 0:
        hits += 1
        ctx = flat[max(0, i - 80): i + len(needle) + 80]
        print("p.%-4d [%s]\n    …%s…" % (n, page_section(n), ctx))
        i = flat.find(needle, i + 1)
print("%d hit(s)" % hits)

codex
The two reports substantially overlap, but one makes several stronger corpus claims (chapter scope, endnote attachment, edition baseline). I’m separating textual claims from judgment calls now so each finding gets an explicit evidence status rather than inheriting the reviewers’ confidence.
