+**CONFIRMED:** corpus pp. 20–21 says this translation uses the altered fourth edition of 1974. The essay repeatedly frames its evidence as a purely “mid-1960s baseline” without warning that its English text is the 1974 state. Harrier service in 1969 and car-carrying SR.N4 service in 1968 were known by then, so they cannot safely be treated as post-text fulfillment.
+
+**UNVERIFIABLE:** the local corpus does not show whether Lem altered the p. 35 VTOL passage between 1964 and 1974. It also lacks the full 1982/1991 self-grades, so the reviewer's assertion that they “do not retract this chapter” cannot be checked and must not be added as fact. State the corpus limit instead.
+
+## 10. The software conclusion is absolute and its quantitative receipt is weak — CONFIRMED; “illegitimate transposition” is REJECTED
+
+**Reviewers:** Grok 8; Agy 5.
+
+**CONFIRMED:** one durable language cannot establish “Software does not go extinct.” The claims register itself warns that the 220–800 billion-line range and transaction shares are unaudited industry estimates, while the prose presents them as scale facts. COBOL supports only the narrower claim that old software can remain load-bearing in the core.
+
+**REJECTED:** Grok's claim that testing p. 38 against software is inherently an unlabeled transposition. Lem applies the analogy to “the totality of man's technical activities,” and the book contract expressly asks about the modern stack. Software is a legitimate 2026 test. The problem is the universal inference and receipt quality, not the choice of test object.
+
+**Required revision:** remove the line-count range and ATM share or explicitly label them industry estimates; change the thesis to “software relics can survive in the centre.”
+
+## 11. Two load-bearing quotations have the wrong page — CONFIRMED
+
+**Reviewer:** Grok 9.
+
+`scripts/find.py` places both “a number of primitive features, as if their birth had been hurried” and “bursts into evolutionary radiation” on p. 34, not p. 33. `checks/quotes.tsv` already has p. 34, which explains why `verify.sh` passes despite the HTML errors.
+
+**Required revision:** change both citations to p. 34.
+
+## 12. The archaeopteryx sentence attributes a rule Lem did not apply — CONFIRMED
+
+**Reviewer:** Agy 6.
+
+Corpus p. 35 uses archaeopteryx only as an example of a poor early form: it “did not as much fly as flutter.” The essay's rule about a lineage under pressure not reinventing its body plan is not stated or applied there. Whether the separate “tinkerer” slogan is associated with François Jacob need not be resolved to see that the attribution to this Lem passage is false.
+
+**Required revision:** cut “He applied that rule to the archaeopteryx” or explicitly label the rule as the essay's inference.
+
+## 13. The reviewers’ factual-challenge bundle needs item-by-item adjudication
+
+**Reviewer:** Grok 10.
+
+- `cobol-production` — **CONFIRMED.** The register itself labels the figures unaudited industry estimates; the prose omits the qualification.
+- `sore-ban-2024` — **CONFIRMED.** The rule covers most newly manufactured small spark-ignition engines in California; existing and second-hand machines remain legal. “The lawnmower is being banned” and “the relic is dying” exceed the receipt.
+- `hovercraft-2000` — **CONFIRMED.** “The air-cushion vehicle is gone” contradicts the essay's own remaining scheduled route and its narrower “large commercial hovercraft effectively vanished.”
+- `am-radio-ev` — **REJECTED as an attack on the dated September figure; CONFIRMED for the later undated repetition.** “By September, 296” is a historical claim and is not falsified by the House total later reaching 317. The later restatement in “Where he was worst” (“a bill with 296 cosponsors”) drops the date and marker and is stale. The official page currently lists 317 House cosponsors and only 60 Senate cosponsors, so the essay's “more than sixty Senate cosponsors” is independently wrong ([House](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/979/cosponsors), [Senate](https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-bill/315/cosponsors)).
+- `alexnet-2012` — **CONFIRMED in part.** The two GTX 580s support the hardware fact, not 2026 dominance, constant acceleration, or unforeseeability.
+- `baseline-1964-ch2` — **REJECTED as stated.** The reviewer silently changes the essay's “cross-Channel hovercraft entering service” into “car-carrying cross-Channel hovercraft.” Those are not the same claim. However, the claims-register source cell supplies no hovercraft source at all; that separate omission needs fixing.
+- `ev-share-2025` — **CONFIRMED as a wording problem only.** If the IEA's electric category includes plug-in hybrids, “three-quarters still burn petrol” should be “at least three-quarters” or should use an ICE-only split. The direction is not challenged.
+- `vtol-aircraft` / `evtol-2026` — **CONFIRMED as unchallenged facts, not as support for the present ledger construction.** Programme dates and certification status do not cure finding 8.
+
+## 14. The reader-persuasion criticism follows from the confirmed ledger asymmetry — CONFIRMED
+
+**Reviewers:** Grok 12; Agy 7.
+
+This is a synthesis rather than independent evidence, but it is fair. The essay is strict with a hedged air-cushion scenario while generous to an existing VTOL prototype, a mismatched AM event, and a compound AlexNet row. “Where Lem was better than us” then praises the very specificity the ledger has blurred. Correcting findings 4–10 would address this without flattening the voice.
+
+## Findings both reviewers missed
+
+### A. `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER` is the wrong verdict form for the software row — CONFIRMED
+
+The p. 38 Lem cell is a declarative claim that the evolutions have similar dynamic laws, not a dilemma plus Lem's proposed resolution. The 2026 cell offers a counterexample. Under `CONTEXT.md` §4 this is not `RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER`; after explicitly labelling software as the essay's test, it is either a narrow `MISS` or should be decomposed into a real problem-level question and answer.
+
+### B. “The car was replaced” is false on the essay's own evidence — CONFIRMED
+
+The first sentence of “What happened” says “The car was replaced.” The paragraph immediately says wheel drive, roads, and the car persist while the propulsion system changes. The intended claim is that the internal-combustion drivetrain is being displaced in new sales, not that the car was replaced.
+
+### C. “Constant acceleration” is strengthened to “acceleration without limit” — CONFIRMED
+
+The third Still Open item says note 6 “predicts acceleration without limit.” Corpus p. 356 says technoevolution's speed is “characterized by constant acceleration”; it does not add “without limit.” Elsewhere in the same chapter, p. 51 expressly discusses limits to technoevolution's speed. Preserve Lem's wording and test whether acceleration continued; do not insert an unbounded claim.
+
+### D. The software section adds two unsupported absolutes beyond “no extinction” — CONFIRMED
+
+“Nothing forces the switch” ignores forced migrations from regulation, vendor support, security, or hardware constraints, none of which the COBOL receipt tests. The Still Open phrase “something that costs nothing to keep running” is also unsupported and implausibly absolute; legacy operation plainly has costs even when replacement costs more. These claims need receipts or deletion.
+
+### E. The claims register does not receipt its hovercraft baseline — CONFIRMED
+
+`baseline-1964-ch2` names “cross-Channel hovercraft entering service,” but its source cell lists de Latil, Lévi-Strauss, NASA, and the P.1127 only. This is exactly the kind of marker/register mismatch `verify.sh` cannot catch: the ID exists, but one factual component has no source.
+
+### F. The Senate cosponsor count is wrong, not merely stale — CONFIRMED
+
+The essay says “more than sixty Senate cosponsors”; Congress.gov lists 60. Grok accepted that line while challenging the House number, so both reviewers missed the direct numerical error. Use “60 Senate cosponsors” with an as-of date, or drop both counts and say the bills attracted broad bipartisan sponsorship.
+
